  "body": "## Summary\nThe Fill-or-Kill (FOK) order functionality in `CLOB.sol` is flawed. It causes transactions to revert if any \"dust\" amount remains after matching, even when this dust is untradeable due to the market's `lotSizeInBase` constraint. This contradicts the expected behavior of a FOK order, which should only fail if the *tradeable* portion cannot be filled completely.\n\n## Finding Description\nA FOK order requires the entire order to be filled immediately and in its entirety, or the transaction must be reverted. The `CLOB.sol` contract implements markets with a `lotSizeInBase`, which is the smallest indivisible unit of the base asset that can be traded.\n\nWhen a taker order is matched against resting limit orders, the fill amount is rounded down to the nearest lot size. This can result in a small, remaining portion of the taker's order that is smaller than one `lotSizeInBase` (i.e., \"dust\"). This dust amount is fundamentally untradeable.\n\nThe issue occurs in the `_processFillBidOrder` and `_processFillAskOrder` functions. After the matching engine runs, the contract performs the following check:\n```solidity\n// in CLOB.sol\nif (args.fillOrderType == FillOrderType.FILL_OR_KILL && newOrder.amount > 0) revert FOKOrderNotFilled();\n```\nThis logic is too simplistic. It reverts if any amount of the order remains (newOrder.amount > 0), failing to distinguish between a failure due to insufficient liquidity and a success where only untradeable dust is left.\n\nScenario:\n1. A market is configured with `lotSizeInBase = 1 ether`.\n2. A resting limit order exists to sell `100 ETH`.\n3. A user submits a FOK order via `postFillOrder` to buy `100.5 ether`.\n4. The matching engine fills `100 ether`. The remaining `newOrder.amount` is `0.5 ether`.\n5. The check `newOrder.amount > 0` evaluates to true, and the transaction incorrectly reverts with `FOKOrderNotFilled()`, failing the user's otherwise successful trade.\n\nThe project's `README.md` even acknowledges that this behavior is not what is intended, confirming the validity of this issue:\n\n```md\nOn CLOB fill, filled amounts are rounded down to the nearest lot. FOK fill orders should not revert if only the amount rounded off is left unfilled, and the user is not charged for the dust.\n\n```\n## Impact Explanation\nThis is a significant correctness bug that severely degrades a core feature of the protocol. The FOK order type becomes unreliable and unusable in practice, as users' valid trades will frequently and unexpectedly fail due to common rounding artifacts. This breaks the functional promise of the FOK order type and harms the user experience, potentially driving users away. While it doesn't cause a direct loss of funds, it prevents the intended functionality of the exchange.\n\n## Likelihood Explanation\n In any active market, it is very common for a taker's desired trade size not to be an exact multiple of the available liquidity's lot sizes. Any user attempting a FOK order in such a common scenario will have their transaction reverted, making the occurrence of this bug highly likely in a live environment.\n\n## Recommendation\nThe FOK validation logic should be updated to be more nuanced. It should only revert if the order was not filled because it hit its price limit or because all liquidity was exhausted. If the only remaining amount is untradeable dust, the order should be considered a success.\n\nThis can be achieved by checking the state of the order book after the matching loop. If there is no more affordable liquidity, then any remaining order amount implies the FOK order has failed. If affordable liquidity still exists, it implies the matching loop was broken by dust, and the FOK order should succeed.\n\n```solidity\n        (uint256 totalQuoteSent, uint256 totalBaseReceived) = _matchIncomingBid(ds, newOrder, args.amountIsBase);\n\n        if (totalQuoteSent == 0 || totalBaseReceived == 0) revert ZeroCostTrade();\n        if (args.fillOrderType == FillOrderType.FILL_OR_KILL && newOrder.amount > 0) {\n            // Revert only if the order was not fully filled because liquidity ran out or the price limit was hit.\n            // If the best ask is now higher than the order's price limit, it means all\n            // affordable liquidity was taken. Any remaining `newOrder.amount` is therefore not dust.\n            if (ds.getBestAskPrice() > newOrder.price) revert FOKOrderNotFilled();\n        }\n\n        // slither-disable-next-line reentrancy-events This external call is to the factory\n        uint256 takerFee = _settleIncomingOrder(ds, account, Side.BUY, totalQuoteSent, totalBaseReceived);\n        (uint256 quoteReceived, uint256 baseSent) = _matchIncomingAsk(ds, newOrder, args.amountIsBase);\n\n        if (quoteReceived == 0 || baseSent == 0) revert ZeroCostTrade();\n        if (args.fillOrderType == FillOrderType.FILL_OR_KILL && newOrder.amount > 0) {\n            // Revert only if the order was not fully filled because liquidity ran out or the price limit was hit.\n            // If the best bid is now lower than the order's price limit, it means all\n            // affordable liquidity was taken. Any remaining `newOrder.amount` is therefore not dust.\n            if (ds.getBestBidPrice() < newOrder.price) revert FOKOrderNotFilled();\n        }\n\n        // slither-disable-next-line reentrancy-events This external call is to the factory\n        uint256 takerFee = _settleIncomingOrder(ds, account, Side.SELL, quoteReceived, baseSent);\n\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "Paste the test into the POC.t.sol file\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.0;\n\nimport {CLOB} from \"contracts/clob/CLOB.sol\";\nimport {ICLOB} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOB.sol\";\nimport {Side} from \"contracts/clob/types/Order.sol\";\nimport {SettingsParams} from \"contracts/clob/ICLOBManager.sol\";\nimport {PoCTestBase} from \"./PoCTestBase.t.sol\";\n\ncontract PoC is PoCTestBase {\n    function test_FOKRevertsOnDust() external {\n        // This test demonstrates the \"Fill-or-Kill (FOK) Orders Revert on Unfillable Dust\" issue.\n        // A FOK order will incorrectly revert if the only unfilled portion is an amount\n        // smaller than the market's lot size (dust), even if all tradeable liquidity was taken.\n\n        // 1. Setup: Create a market with a lot size of 1 WETH.\n        SettingsParams memory settings;\n        settings.owner = address(this);\n        settings.tickSize = 1e12; // Price tick size\n        settings.lotSizeInBase = 1 ether; // Minimum tradeable unit is 1 WETH\n        settings.minLimitOrderAmountInBase = 1 ether;\n        settings.maxLimitsPerTx = 10;\n        clob = CLOB(payable(clobManager.createMarket(address(weth), address(USDC), settings)));\n\n        // 2. Create and fund two users: a maker and a taker.\n        address maker = makeAddr(\"maker\");\n        address taker = makeAddr(\"taker\");\n\n        // Fund maker with 10 WETH for the limit order\n        deal(address(weth), maker, 10 ether);\n        vm.startPrank(maker);\n        weth.approve(address(accountManager), 10 ether);\n        accountManager.deposit(maker, address(weth), 10 ether);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Fund taker with enough USDC to fill the order\n        deal(address(USDC), taker, 21_000 * 1e6); // USDC has 6 decimals\n        vm.startPrank(taker);\n        USDC.approve(address(accountManager), 21_000 * 1e6);\n        accountManager.deposit(taker, address(USDC), 21_000 * 1e6);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // 3. Maker places a limit order to sell exactly 10 WETH at a price of 2000 USDC.\n        vm.startPrank(maker);\n        ICLOB.PostLimitOrderArgs memory limitArgs;\n        limitArgs.side = Side.SELL;\n        limitArgs.price = 2000 * 1e12; // Price must be scaled by tickSize\n        limitArgs.amountInBase = 10 ether; // Exactly 10 lots\n        limitArgs.limitOrderType = ICLOB.LimitOrderType.POST_ONLY;\n        clob.postLimitOrder(maker, limitArgs);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // 4. Taker places a FOK order to buy 10.5 WETH.\n        // This should fill 10 WETH and leave 0.5 WETH as untradeable dust.\n        // The current implementation incorrectly reverts instead of succeeding.\n        vm.startPrank(taker);\n        ICLOB.PostFillOrderArgs memory fillArgs;\n        fillArgs.side = Side.BUY;\n        fillArgs.amount = 10.5 ether; // The amount includes dust (0.5 ether)\n        fillArgs.amountIsBase = true;\n        fillArgs.priceLimit = 2000 * 1e12; // Price is sufficient to fill\n        fillArgs.fillOrderType = ICLOB.FillOrderType.FILL_OR_KILL;\n\n        vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(CLOB.FOKOrderNotFilled.selector));\n        clob.postFillOrder(taker, fillArgs);\n    }\n}\n```",
